{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Round-4 plan review BLOCK addressed. Finding: the plan claimed a fail-closed macOS posture, but probe() detected sandbox-exec by PATH alone while verifying bwrap functionally — the exact asymmetry the probe's own docstring argues against, three lines above the check. Since sandbox-exec is Apple-deprecated, a non-enforcing stub would satisfy shutil.which, exit 0, and run every mutation effectively UNSANDBOXED on macOS (the platform the incident happened on) with no abort.\n\nGerrit change 2276 (MERGED) adds _seatbelt_works(), symmetric with _bwrap_works(): it runs a real sandboxed write under a deny-all profile and requires BOTH a liveness marker on stdout AND the file's absence. Absence alone is not denial — a sandbox that failed to launch leaves the file absent too.\n\nProven RED by mutation: reverting to the PATH-only check fails the stub-detection test; keying on exit status instead of observed denial fails it; dropping the liveness half fails the never-launched test; removing shlex.quote on the probe target fails the spaced-TMPDIR test. The real macOS sandbox-exec still probes true, so the working path is unchanged.\n\nThree defects were caught during this change and fixed before landing: (1) the pre-existing probe-order test stubbed only shutil.which and would have FAILED on Linux, where /usr/bin/sandbox-exec does not exist — reproduced locally as 'assert None == seatbelt'; (2) the return-True branch had no non-@live coverage, so it ran on no Linux gate cell; (3) the probe interpolated its target into /bin/sh -c unquoted, so a TMPDIR containing a space would fail the redirect for a non-sandbox reason and report a denial that never happened.\n\nOne CI cell (ubuntu py3.11) failed environmentally — a Gerrit fetch timeout in the checkout step at 29s, no test executed — while py3.12, py3.13 and macOS passed on identical code. Rechecked with that reasoning recorded on the change; the rerun was green."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787872760893649001,"uuid":"c83d9724-9360-41ee-a511-8994f9ab19b8"}